Stepped onto this while trying to load a MusicGen onnx model into candle (just the text encoder part). This was also reported in https://github.com/huggingface/candle/issues/1305 for another onnx model, so I decided to give it a shot.

This code replicates the problem described in https://github.com/huggingface/candle/issues/1305:
```rust
use candle_core::{DType, Device, Tensor};
use std::collections::HashMap;

fn main() -> candle_core::Result<()> {
    // onnx model from https://github.com/huggingface/candle/issues/1305#issuecomment-1806795045
    let model = candle_onnx::read_file("speedyspeech.onnx")?; 
    let graph = model.graph.as_ref().unwrap();

    let input_1 = graph.input[0].name.to_string();
    let input_2 = graph.input[1].name.to_string();

    let mut inputs = HashMap::new();
    inputs.insert(input_1, Tensor::zeros((1, 16), DType::I64, &Device::Cpu)?);
    inputs.insert(input_2, Tensor::zeros(1, DType::I64, &Device::Cpu)?);

    candle_onnx::simple_eval(&model, inputs)?;
    Ok(())
}
```
Which throws the following error:
```
not yet implemented: implement gather for Tensor[dims 78, 128; f32] Tensor[dims 1, 16; i64] axis 0
```

With this PR, that error is solved, but another error that seems unrelated (I think) appears:
```
Error: DTypeMismatchBinaryOp { lhs: I64, rhs: F32, op: "cat" }
```

The main challenge is that the [onnx "Gather" specification](https://onnx.ai/onnx/operators/onnx__Gather.html) is completely different from `Tensor::gather` in candle, and there does not seem to be any 1:1 operation that resembles onnx's "Gather". I came around with a solution that uses `index_select` for the cases where `inidices` has a dimension of 1 and 2, which makes the tests go green and should be suitable for most use-cases.

That's the best that I could get, some guidance about better options is much appreciated.

---

**Additional piece of info**: I was also able to solve the problem while importing MusicGen's text encoder onnx model, just to step into this other problem:
```
Error: unsupported op_type ReduceMean for op NodeProto
```

Added types for representing account transactions with "transfer" being the only implemented payload type yet. The types are arranged such that you go from expressing an "intent" (i.e. to transfer C from X to Y) with `AccountTransaction` to including expiry, cost, and signatures in a logical sequence. This will end out with a `SignedAccountTransaction` which can be sent using `NodeClient`.

Convenient `sign` methods have been added for going directly from `AccountTransaction` to `SignedAccountTransaction`, such that all you have to write is

```swift
let tx = AccountTransaction(
    sender: myAccount.address,
    payload: .transfer(amount: myAmount, receiver: myReceiver)
)
// fetch next sequence number (nextSeq) of sender account
let signedTx = myAccount.keys.sign(tx, sequenceNumber: nextSeq, expiry: myExpiry)
```

See `Tests/ConcordiumTests/WalletTest.swift` for the spelled out version.

Decoding and decryption of the Legacy Wallet export file format were also implemented to make it easy to interact with legacy accounts.

Commands for performing transfers using either legacy or seed based accounts were added to the example CLI.
